WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.820
New mapping shows snake-inventoming affects far more people than official records indicate. This is Planetterrian Daily, episode 212. Most people assume menopause is a gradual hormone fade that stretches across a decade. 300 million laboratory tests from more than 1 million women show multiple organ systems flipping at the final menstrual period itself. The analysis discussed on the science subreddit, tracks bone turnover markers, liver enzymes, kidney filtration measures, and blood lipid profiles.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:34.600
cells. Those four sets of markers all register the change inside the same narrow window.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.720
The timing lines up with ovarian estrogen production dropping below a functional threshold.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.560
Until that drop, estrogen had buffered bone, liver, kidney, and lipid systems with a steady

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:51.480
regulatory signal. Hormone shifts that predict the transition can begin up to 15 years earlier.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:57.360
The discontinuity at the last period is still abrupt. Men of the same age, tracked in the

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.760
same datasets show no comparable jump.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:04.920
The sample is large enough to separate that menopause link discontinuity from ordinary

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:06.060
age-related drift.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:12.000
Targeted panels of bone density, liver enzymes, and lipid profiles can be timed to the

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.460
months around a patient's reported final period.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.340
That is a tighter window than spreading the same checks evenly across the preceding decade.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:25.360
Columbia scientists identified a bacterial antiviral pathway that protects genomes from viral

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:25.740
attack.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:31.020
Fizz.org reports the system shares molecular features with processes that safeguard

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:36.200
eukaryotic chromosomes. The same mechanism illuminates an early evolutionary event

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:41.100
that contributed to the emergence of higher organisms. The finding connects microbial

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:47.020
immunity to genome stability machinery across domains of life. Researchers suggest the pathway

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:52.620
could be repurposed for targeted DNA editing in human cells. They also describe a possible

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:57.900
adaptation into a gene editor aimed at slowing cellular aging and improving cancer therapies.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:04.340
A geostatistical model now estimates snake inventing and fatality rates across 79 countries.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:11.500
Researchers in work covered by Fizz.org reviewed studies through March 2025 and folded in climate,

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:18.440
snake species richness, and socioeconomic data. Sub-Saharan Africa carries the heaviest estimated toll,

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:22.420
multiple, more than half of at-risk nations lack systematic case recording.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.920
Many victims live hours from clinics or see traditional healers first, so those cases

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.620
never international statistics.

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:34.940
The framework combines real observations with environmental signals to produce country-level

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:35.460
estimates.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:41.360
Elephant mega-carcasses leave nutrient legacies that create cold spots of biodiversity beside

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.460
hot spots of ecosystem processes.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:48.940
A study summarized on the science subreddit maps how large animal decomposition alters

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:52.820
soil chemistry and plant communities over time in savannah landscapes.

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:58.360
As the body breaks down, nitrogen, phosphorus, and other minerals collect in tight soil

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:00.060
patches and stay there for years.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:05.980
Nearby, some microbes and plants speed up, while the count of species in that immediate zone

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:06.360
falls.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:11.860
A single carcass site can reorganize food webs and nutrient cycling long after the animal

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:12.360
is gone.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:17.620
on. Persistent nutrient gradients can suppress some species while speeding decomposition and

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:22.380
nutrient cycling in others. Similar inputs likely occurred across continents before

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:28.000
megafauna declines cut how often they arrived. The study leaves open what scale of carcass

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:33.320
input would shift biodiversity metrics in a given savanna patch, and whether restoring large

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:39.360
herbivore populations could recreate these process hotspots. Dr. Ruth Thurston at the University

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:46.120
of Exeter read 1,192 seafood recipes in good housekeeping, starting in 1923.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:51.560
Fizz.org reports that before the 1970s, the pages featured diverse species

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:57.380
with flatfish, herring, and oysters prominent. After the 1970s, five species,

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:04.700
cod, haddock, tuna, salmon, and shrimp, rose to roughly 80% of recipes and other species dropped.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:10.620
The shift lined up with falling United Kingdom catches, rising imports, supermarket growth

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:16.560
and frozen standardized products. Early recipes stress domestic skills and whole fish preparation.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:23.760
Later ones emphasize convenience and status. Herring, oyster and lobster remain available, yet they receive

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:29.800
far less attention in published guidance. Incorrect outputs from medical chatbots can prompt patient decisions

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:32.720
whose origins rarely show up in clinical records.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.700
An analysis discussed on the science subreddit

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:38.620
follows how those errors move through self-directed actions

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:41.920
that patients may not report as coming from automated advice.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:45.060
Standard medical documentation rarely records

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:48.020
the source of information that led to a particular choice.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:51.100
Attributing harm is hard when the first recommendation

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:52.780
leaves no clear audit trail.

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:55.620
The GAP complicates individual case review

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:58.640
and broader safety monitoring of consumer facing tools.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:04.460
• Dr. Sheamus O'Mehani, a gastroenterologist with over 30 years in National Health Service

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:10.780
or NHS Hospitals, argues that drugs, stem cells, and epigenetic reprogramming have not delayed

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:11.720
aging in humans.

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:17.400
The telegraph reports his view that diet and exercise reduce disease risk and extend healthy

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:21.500
years, and that the distinction matters for claims about outweeting aging.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:27.300
His new book examines the cultural and financial momentum around longevity summits and biotech

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:27.780
investment.

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:33.640
He first encountered the movement at the 2024 longevity summit in Dublin, where Aubrey

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:37.560
DeGrey presented ideas about avoiding age-related death through technology.

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:43.460
Omajani observes that older people were once valued for knowledge, yet now often live in

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:45.840
nursing homes where death is feared more intensely.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:51.440
The book interviews leading figures and asks whether the billion-dollar industry rests on demonstrated

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:52.280
human results.

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:57.120
adults. Dr. David Sinclair notes that nearly every adult cell still holds the genetic

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:03.020
instructions used to build an arm before birth. Lifespan.com describes how those instructions

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:08.860
produce bones of the right length, movable joints, muscles, tendons, blood vessels, skin

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:13.860
and nerves connected to the spine. The body ran that construction twice during embryonic

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:18.840
development, without external scaffolds or models. In adults, the same instructions

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:23.140
stay silent after limb loss, and wounds closed through scar tissue instead.

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:28.040
Adult cells retain the full genome, yet do not restart that morphogenetic sequence

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:29.140
after amputation.

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:34.520
Sinclair's work tests whether partial cellular rejuvenation can reactivate those developmental

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:36.120
programs and mature tissues.

